/*!

@page QMMF_Recorder_Bufferflow Bufferflow

__Buffer flow for Video__
@image html /Images/Buffer_flow_for_Video_Diagram.png width=900px

___

__Buffer flow for Capture and YUV__
@image html /Images/Buffers_flow_capture_YUV_Diagram.png width=600px


___


### Buffer allocation: ###


Allocation of buffers is handled by Camera Adaptor, Post-processing pipe
and Encoder.

+   Buffers allocated by Camera Adaptor are used by Camera, Post-processing
pipe, Encoder and Client.
+   Buffers allocated by Post-processing pipe are used by Post-processing pipe
itself, Encoder and Client.
+   Encoder allocates internal work buffers and buffers for encoded video.

### Buffer flow: ###

+   Camera adaptor allocates buffers per device stream. Maximum number of
buffers are set while creation of device stream. Buffers are not allocated
during the creation. Camera adaptor queries buffers by __GetBuffer__ API from
each device stream while preparing Capture request. Device stream will allocate
buffer if there is no free buffer and the maximum number of buffers is not
reached. It means that device stream buffers are allocated on demand.
__GetBuffer__ Device stream API will block until client returns buffer or if
maximum number of buffers is reached.

+   Camera Adaptor sends buffers to Camera HAL with Capture request. The Camera
fills those buffers and returns them to Camera Adaptor by result callback. Then
Camera Adaptor forwards them to Device stream client. The client might be
Post-processing pipe, Video Encoder or QMMF Recorder Client.

+   Buffers in post-processing are allocated on demand similarly to buffers in
Camera Adaptor. Post-Processing node allocates output buffers when new
processing is triggered. Buffers are allocated if there is no free buffer from
previous iteration or maximum number of buffers is reached. Last node in the
Post-processing pipe sends buffers to Video Encoder or Client.

+   Encoder allocates buffers when instances are created. Encoder forwards
output buffers to the QMMF Recorder client.

### Tuning number of buffers ###

+   __Camera adaptor buffers__ - Camera adaptor buffers are used by Camera and
Camera adaptor client. Both of them set the maximum number of buffers and camera
adaptor takes the bigger number. If buffers are not enough, this leads to frame
rate drop. If buffers are more than required extra buffers will never be
allocated because buffers are allocated on demand. Still, small optimization is
possible. For example if client returns buffer 5 ms later than __GetBuffer__ is
called one extra buffer will be allocated. But if we tune the maximum number of
buffers __GetBuffer__ API will wait 5 ms instead of allocating new buffer. In
some cases frame rate will not be impacted, but we will save one buffer.


+   __Post-processing buffers__ - The Post-processing pipe has parameters which
clip the number of internal and output buffers. If parameter is not set,
Post-processing pipe will allocate enough buffers so all plugins can run in
parallel. This will guarantee minimum latency. But if memory usage is more
important than the number of internal buffers can be clipped. The
Post-processing pipe will work even with one buffer, but this will increase
processing latency.

+   __Encoder buffers__ - Encoder buffers cannot be tuned.

___
*/